  message: "DEFERRED in the G11 sweep. WI-gojum ('rust-analyzer as the accurate Rust engine — multi-fidelity reconciliation, ADR-0012 Steps 2-3') is a PARKED architectural initiative: reconciling the two Rust backends' outputs (tree-sitter rust.py + rust-analyzer SCIP) into a single higher-fidelity view is a large multi-PR design effort gated on ADR-0012 Steps 2-3, not a 'to the extent practical' quick fix. The identity/parity plumbing between the backends is now sound (WI-kilih #14 closed the last stable_id-parity gap), so the two coexist correctly; full multi-fidelity RECONCILIATION (choosing/merging when both emit for the same symbol) remains the parked design work. Left as-is (parked); reopen when ADR-0012 Steps 2-3 are prioritized. G11 tractable content = WI-kilih (done #14); WI-gojum parked-deferred."  # ca05
- op: discuss  # 5f03
  at: '2026-09-17T23:33:17Z'  # 5f03
  by: agent  # 5f03
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 5f03
  clock: 3  # 5f03
  nonce: 5f03  # 5f03
  message: "READINESS OBSERVATION handed over from WI-jivim, per that row's own\n2026-08-23 self-correction (\"drop the dedup half to WI-gojum, where the parked\ndesign lives\"). WI-jivim is now closed on its filed core.\n\nTHE POINT, narrowly. ADR-0012 coexistence is parked and duplicates are\nEXPECTED, so this is not a defect claim. But stable-id parity is offered as\nREADINESS for a future dedup, and on real output the keys do not line up for\nany dedup to use:\n\n    rust:src/lib.rs:11-13:load_config:function     <- tree-sitter\n    rust:src/lib.rs:11-11:load_config:method       <- scip\n\nThey differ in BOTH the kind slot and the span, so no dedup key over\n(path, span, name, kind) can match them. Two independent mismatches:\n\n1. KIND. SCIP's DescriptorKind.METHOD covers a Rust FREE FUNCTION as well as a\n   method -- ``load_config().`` parses as METHOD because of the ``(...)``\n   disambiguator -- so the importer maps both onto Symbol.kind=\"method\" while\n   tree-sitter calls the free one \"function\". The descriptor CHAIN carries the\n   answer (a parent TYPE descriptor means method, a parent NAMESPACE means free\n   function) and the importer does not consult it.\n\n2. SPAN. tree-sitter spans the whole definition (11-13); SCIP gives the\n   definition OCCURRENCE range (11-11). These are different facts, not a bug in\n   either, so parity needs a rule about which one a dedup key uses.\n\nSIZING, measured at 1aa5b46b1b on ~/whole_bunch_of_repos/aardvark-dns: 79\nmethod-kind scip nodes alongside the tree-sitter functions, in an arm that\ntakes the artifact from 581 to 1255 nodes.\n\nRELATED, and it bears on whether the future dedup is worth building: the same\ntwo-arm run found ZERO call-family edges differing between arms (453 in both)\nand zero receivers typed differently. All 637 scip edges are `references`.\nRecorded in full on WI-jivim.\n\nNo action requested here; filed so the parked design has the measurement when\nit is unparked."  # 5f03
- op: discuss  # c9b2
  at: '2026-09-18T08:40:39Z'  # c9b2
  by: agent  # c9b2
  actor: jgstern_agent  # c9b2
  clock: 4  # c9b2
  nonce: c9b2  # c9b2
  message: "PREMISE CORRECTION 2026-09-18, measured at d6174d5df9. This row's own framing\noverstates what exists, and two of its three sub-components are in a worse state\nthan recorded.\n\n=== \"stable_id parity is readiness for a future dedup\" -- THE READINESS DOES NOT WORK ===\n\nMeasured on aardvark-dns with the backend active: 669 SCIP nodes and 148\ntree-sitter Rust nodes coexist, 52 (path, name) pairs are emitted by BOTH arms,\nand **ZERO of the 52 share a stable_id.** Not few. None.\n\nCause: rust_scip.py requires an exact match on BOTH span endpoints, while\nproduction supplies the SCIP Definition-Occurrence range -- the identifier token\n(5-5) -- never the enclosing function_item (5-30). Every lookup misses and the\nSCIP-derived id is retained. The helper's own docstring says the opposite of the\npredicate three lines below it (\"Column precision is not required: a function\ndefinition is uniquely identified by its start line\").\n\nThe parity test cannot catch this: it collects a baseline from rust.py and feeds\nthose same spans back into the helper, so it proves the helper agrees with rust.py\non rust.py's input and says nothing about SCIP-shaped input.\n\nAnd it would be undone if it did fire: split_within_file_stable_id_collisions\ngroups on (path, stable_id) and RE-MINTS the second member, because it reads such\na group as \"distinct code SITES\". Two arms agreeing is exactly the shape it exists\nto break apart.\n\nSo the accurate statement is not \"readiness exists but is unwired\". It is:\n**the readiness is non-functional on production input, untested against\nproducer-shaped input, and structurally opposed by a later pass.** Filed as its\nown invariant row (2026-09-18) with the fix shapes.\n\n=== \"the dedup using it is not wired into the survey pipeline\" -- IT WAS NEVER WRITTEN ===\n\nStronger than unwired. git log -S over the whole history finds no commit that\nadds, moves or removes a cross-pass symbol merge/dedup/reconcile pass. Only seven\ncommits in history touch the WI-bajuz string, and none of them is an\nimplementation; its surviving homes are two docstrings, two docs files and one\ntest. Symbols are accumulated by a bare all_symbols.extend() with no key of any\nkind. Edges ARE deduped, but on endpoint Symbol.ids, which differ between arms,\nso that path can never collapse a cross-backend pair.\n\nNot removed, not broken, not disabled -- never implemented.\n\n=== THE ADR CITATION THIS ROW RESTS ON DOES NOT EXIST ===\n\nThis row states \"ADR-0012's intended multi-fidelity design is COEXISTENCE ... a\nreplace design would AMEND ADR-0012.\" Verified by term over ADR-0012:\nscip 0, rust-analyzer 0, dedup 0. Its two \"coexist\" uses are about two analyzer\nREGISTRATION systems and about two EDGES at one callsite -- neither is a ruling\non duplicate SYMBOLS. Meanwhile the spec asserts coexistence as settled current\nbehaviour citing \"ADR-0012 coexistence; WI-gojum\", and\ndocs/future/scip-generalization-vision.md lists coexistence-vs-replacement as an\nOPEN question. Three documents, three statuses. Filed as its own\nneeds_human_review row (2026-09-18) because writing or amending the ADR is the\nowner's call.\n\n=== WHAT THIS MEANS FOR UNPARKING ===\n\nThis row's reopen trigger is \"when ADR-0012 Steps 2-3 are prioritized\". Two\nthings should be true before that happens, and neither is today:\n\n  1. The coexist-vs-replace question has an actual decision document.\n  2. The parity either works or is retracted. Unparking onto a mechanism that\n     measures 0/52 would build reconciliation on a foundation that is not there.\n\nAlso note the ARM's measured contribution, because it bears on whether\nreconciliation is worth building: the SCIP arm changes ZERO call edges (453 in\nboth arms on aardvark-dns, 0 differing) and types ZERO receivers (WI-jivim), and\n71.4% of its edges point at function-locals of which 329 are provably false\ncross-file references (filed 2026-09-18). Post-drop it would contribute 169 nodes\nand 182 `references` edges. That is a thin base for a reconciliation design, and\nit is a fair question whether the arm earns its keep before it earns a merge pass."  # c9b2
